WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.965
Kaplan 2020: loss is a power law.

2
00:00:03.245 --> 00:00:11.267
In January 2020, Jared Kaplan and colleagues at OpenAI published “Scaling Laws for Neural Language Models.

3
00:00:11.547 --> 00:00:20.336
” They trained many transformer language models of different sizes, on different amounts of data, with different compute budgets, and measured test loss.

4
00:00:20.616 --> 00:00:30.899
Their finding was that loss followed smooth power laws in each of the three factors, across more than seven orders of magnitude, as long as the other two were not the bottleneck.

5
00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:38.219
Architectural details such as depth versus width mattered much less than raw scale within the range they tested.

6
00:00:38.499 --> 00:00:41.251
Two features of the paper shaped the next few years.

7
00:00:41.531 --> 00:00:51.728
First, the curves were smooth and predictable, so a lab could train small models, fit the line, and forecast what a much larger run would achieve before spending the money.

8
00:00:52.008 --> 00:00:57.704
That predictability is why scaling became an investment thesis and not just a research result.

9
00:00:57.984 --> 00:01:10.016
Second, the paper’s reading of the optimal trade-off favored making models bigger faster than giving them more data: for a fixed compute budget, it suggested spending most of the increase on parameters.

10
00:01:10.296 --> 00:01:16.227
Notice what the paper measured: cross-entropy loss on text from the same distribution as training.

11
00:01:16.507 --> 00:01:21.968
It did not measure reasoning, factual accuracy, coding ability, or any downstream task.

12
00:01:22.248 --> 00:01:31.955
Loss is a good leading indicator, and lower loss has tended to come with better capabilities, but the link between the two is itself empirical and uneven.

13
00:01:32.235 --> 00:01:39.509
Some abilities improve smoothly with scale; others appear to jump; some barely move until post-training targets them.
